Bug-fix and performance patch for CoMSA 1.1
===========================================

Applies to: CoMSA v1.1 (https://github.com/refresh-bio/CoMSA/releases/tag/v1.1,
            source archive .../archive/refs/tags/v1.1.tar.gz). It does NOT apply to
            the untagged 1.2 code on CoMSA's master branch.
Apply with: cd CoMSA-1.1 && patch -p1 < comsa-1.1-fixes.patch && make
License:    GPL-3, like CoMSA itself.

The archive format is unchanged: for valid input the alignment stream is
byte-identical to unpatched CoMSA 1.1, and archives decode with either build.
The only exception is '~', which 1.1 could not handle and which patched archives
encode with a new symbol rank.

Fixes
- Input validation: sequence symbols must be A-Z, a-z, '-', '.', '*' or '~'. In
  1.1, '}' was silently corrupted, '~' produced archives that crashed the
  decompressor, and digits, '?' and non-ASCII bytes aborted compression. '~' (Easel's
  missing-data symbol) is now supported. Unequal sequence lengths and duplicate
  sequence names (interleaved Stockholm, not supported) give a clear error instead
  of exit(1) from a worker thread.
- An input file whose size is a multiple of 128 MiB no longer loops until memory
  runs out (CInFile::Get end-of-file handling).
- Damaged or truncated archives fail cleanly instead of hanging or crashing: the
  footer, block sizes, varints, LZMA result and range-decoder state are all
  checked.
- The exit status is non-zero on failure, and the partial output file is removed.
- The @list file is read with getline (no fixed-size buffer; names may contain
  spaces). Reading of #=GF ID/AC no longer reads past short lines. The no-op
  assert() in the entropy decoder is now a real check. Write errors are reported.
  Families whose sequences are all empty round-trip. FASTA files without a
  trailing newline no longer lose their last byte. Fixed a decoder memory leak.
- Size fields are written explicitly as little-endian (same bytes on x86/ARM).

Performance
- The WFC weight table is built once instead of once per family per thread (it
  was ~60% of the CPU time on small families).
- The LZMA dictionary is sized to the input instead of always 64 MiB.
- Families are compressed and decompressed in parallel (new option -t <threads>).
  Small families run their pipeline stages in the calling thread instead of
  starting ~9 threads each.
- Queue items are moved instead of copied; PBWT histograms are allocated once.
- Typical effect: 3,000 small Pfam families compress 15.3 s -> 0.34 s and
  decompress 7.8 s -> 0.25 s.

